 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+Use a resident 2Q policy with two byte-accounted queues:
+
+- The probationary queue is FIFO and initially receives every completed sheet. A cache hit while a sheet is probationary promotes it to the protected queue.
+- The protected queue is LRU. A cache hit while a sheet is protected moves it to the most-recently-used position.
+
+The initial split reserves 3 GiB for probationary sheets and 9 GiB for protected sheets. This gives one-use screening traffic a bounded admission window while reserving most of the cache for sheets that demonstrate reuse. The split is private worker configuration so it can be tuned from observed hit rates without changing the preview API or render queue.
+
+Admission that exceeds the probationary budget evicts probationary sheets in FIFO order. Promotion that exceeds the protected budget evicts protected sheets in LRU order. Queue membership changes and file admission or deletion occur under the worker's cache lock so requests cannot observe metadata that names a missing file. A completed sheet larger than the probationary budget is served from temporary output but is not admitted to the cache.
+
+Replacement metadata is rebuilt from an empty state when the worker starts; cache files without live queue membership are removed. This avoids treating filesystem timestamps as policy history and prevents stale files from consuming the cache budget.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|-------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache replacement policy | [Resident 2Q with a probationary FIFO and protected LRU](decisions/cache-replacement-policy.md) | Global LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | 2Q prevents a long sequence of one-use screening sheets from displacing sheets that editors have reused for comparison, without adding counters or changing any external contract. |
+| Initial queue budget | 3 GiB probationary and 9 GiB protected, measured by JPEG bytes | Equal split; entry-count limits; a larger probationary share | A 25/75 split reserves most capacity for demonstrated reuse while leaving room for roughly 1,500 typical 2 MiB sheets in a sequential screening window; byte accounting preserves the 12 GiB bound when sheet sizes vary. |
+
